9. How do you scale applications in Kubernetes?

Scaling Pods: To scale the number of Pods in a Deployment, use the kubectl scale command:

kubectl scale deployment <deployment-name> --replicas=<desired-replica-count>

This adjusts the number of Pods in the Deployment to the specified number.

Horizontal Pod Autoscaling (HPA): Kubernetes supports automatic scaling of Pods based on observed CPU utilization or other custom metrics. The Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) automatically scales the Pods in a Deployment or ReplicaSet. To enable HPA:

Ensure metrics-server is installed in your cluster.

Use the kubectl autoscale command:

kubectl autoscale deployment <deployment-name> --cpu-percent=<target-cpu-utilization> --min=<min-replicas> --max=<max-replicas>
The HPA will scale Pods up or down depending on CPU usage or custom metrics.
10. How does Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) work?

HPA Overview: The Horizontal Pod Autoscaler automatically adjusts the number of Pods in a Deployment, ReplicaSet, or StatefulSet based on observed metrics such as CPU utilization, memory usage, or custom application metrics.

How it works:

Metrics: The HPA controller queries the metrics API (usually provided by the metrics-server or custom metrics providers).
Decision Process: The HPA compares the current CPU utilization or other specified metric to the target value (e.g., 80% CPU). If the current metric exceeds the target, HPA will scale up the number of Pods; if it is below the target, it will scale down.
Scaling Behavior: The scaling action is done gradually, and Kubernetes ensures that at least a minimum number of Pods are always running.

Example:

kubectl autoscale deployment <deployment-name> --cpu-percent=80 --min=1 --max=10
11. What happens when a Kubernetes node goes down?
Pod Rescheduling: If a node goes down, Kubernetes will detect that the Pods running on that node are no longer available. The Kubernetes scheduler will then reschedule those Pods onto other available nodes in the cluster, provided there are enough resources.
Impact on Services:
If there is a ReplicaSet: Pods running as part of a ReplicaSet or Deployment will be rescheduled to other nodes, ensuring that the desired state (number of replicas) is maintained.
If there is no ReplicaSet: The Pod may be lost permanently unless backed by a StatefulSet or Persistent Volumes.
Node Affinity: If the Pods have node affinity rules, they will only be scheduled to nodes that meet those rules, which may cause issues if no suitable nodes are available.
12. How do you check logs of a Pod in Kubernetes?

To view logs from a specific Pod's container, use the kubectl logs command:

kubectl logs <pod-name> -c <container-name>
If the Pod has only one container, you can omit the -c <container-name> part.

For previous container logs, if a container has crashed and restarted, you can use the --previous flag:

kubectl logs <pod-name> -c <container-name> --previous
If you need to view logs for multiple containers in a Pod, run the kubectl logs command for each container individually.

You can also stream logs in real-time using:

kubectl logs -f <pod-name> -c <container-name>
13. How do you expose a Kubernetes application externally?

Service: You can expose an application externally using a Kubernetes Service of type LoadBalancer or NodePort. A LoadBalancer will create an external IP that routes traffic to the service, while NodePort exposes the service on a static port across all nodes in the cluster.

Example of a Service of type LoadBalancer:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: my-service
spec:
  selector:
    app: my-app
  ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 80
      targetPort: 8080
  type: LoadBalancer
The LoadBalancer type creates a cloud provider load balancer, which routes external traffic to your service.

Other methods:

Ingress: You can use an Ingress resource with an Ingress Controller to expose multiple services through a single external IP, often with path-based routing.
14. How do you manage configuration changes in Kubernetes?

ConfigMaps and Secrets: Use ConfigMaps to store non-sensitive configuration data and Secrets for sensitive data like passwords or API keys. Both can be referenced inside Pods through environment variables or mounted as files.

Creating a ConfigMap:

kubectl create configmap <configmap-name> --from-literal=<key>=<value>

Using in Pods:

envFrom:
  - configMapRef:
      name: <configmap-name>
Secrets are similar to ConfigMaps but are base64 encoded and used for storing sensitive information.
Helm: Use Helm charts for managing applications and configurations, especially when managing complex applications with multiple configurable parameters.
15. How do you secure access to Kubernetes cluster?

RBAC (Role-Based Access Control): Use RBAC policies to control access within the Kubernetes cluster. Create roles and role bindings to define permissions at the namespace or cluster level.

Example of a Role and RoleBinding:

kind: Role
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
metadata:
  namespace: <namespace-name>
  name: <role-name>
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods"]
  verbs: ["get", "list"]
Authentication: Ensure strong authentication using methods like client certificates, OpenID Connect, or integrating with an identity provider (LDAP, Active Directory).
Audit Logging: Enable audit logging to track all interactions with the Kubernetes API server.
Network Policies: Restrict communication between Pods using Network Policies, ensuring that only authorized Pods can communicate with each other.
16. How do you manage secrets in Kubernetes?

Kubernetes Secrets: Kubernetes provides a way to store and manage sensitive information in the form of Secrets. These can be created from literal values, files, or external sources like a vault.

Example:

kubectl create secret generic my-secret --from-literal=password=secret

Mounting Secrets: Secrets can be accessed by Pods as environment variables or mounted as files.

Example of mounting a Secret as an environment variable:

env:
  - name: DB_PASSWORD
    valueFrom:
      secretKeyRef:
        name: my-secret
        key: password
Encryption: Ensure that Secrets are encrypted at rest in the Kubernetes cluster. Enable encryption at rest by configuring kube-apiserver to use an encryption provider.
17. How do you monitor a Kubernetes cluster in production?
Prometheus and Grafana: These tools are widely used to monitor Kubernetes clusters. Prometheus collects metrics, while Grafana provides visualization.
Kube-state-metrics: Exposes Kubernetes cluster-level metrics, such as the number of Pods running, their statuses, and resource usage.

kubectl top: This is part of the metrics-server and can be used to view CPU and memory usage of Pods and Nodes.

kubectl top pod --namespace=<namespace-name>
Logging: Use a centralized logging system such as ELK Stack (Elasticsearch, Logstash, and Kibana) or Fluentd to aggregate logs from the Pods and nodes.
18. How do you debug DNS issues inside Kubernetes?

DNS Resolution in Pods: To check if DNS is working inside the Pod, you can use nslookup or dig commands to see if the DNS is resolving properly.

kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- nslookup <service-name>

CoreDNS Logs: CoreDNS is the default DNS service in Kubernetes. Check the logs of the CoreDNS Pods to investigate potential issues:

kubectl logs -n kube-system <coredns-pod-name>
Check DNS Configurations: Ensure that the DNS configurations in your Pod are correct. Kubernetes creates DNS records for Services automatically.


19. What happens if a container crashes repeatedly inside a Pod?

CrashLoopBackOff: If a container inside a Pod crashes repeatedly, Kubernetes will attempt to restart it based on the container's restart policy. After a certain number of failures, Kubernetes will enter a CrashLoopBackOff state, meaning it will back off and stop restarting the container for a period of time to avoid overloading the system.

How to debug:

Check the logs: First, check the logs of the container using kubectl logs <pod-name> -c <container-name>.
Inspect events: Run kubectl describe pod <pod-name> to view detailed information about the Pod's lifecycle events and any error messages that could explain why the container is crashing.
Check resource limits: Ensure that the Pod’s resource limits (memory, CPU) are not too low, as insufficient resources could lead to container crashes.
Investigate liveness/readiness probes: Misconfigured liveness or readiness probes might cause Kubernetes to incorrectly restart a container. Ensure they are correctly set.

Possible Solutions:

Fix application issues: Sometimes, the crash is caused by bugs or misconfigurations in the application itself. Review the application logs and ensure it’s correctly configured.
Adjust restart policy: For testing purposes, you can temporarily adjust the restartPolicy to Never or OnFailure to prevent Kubernetes from trying to restart the container and allow for debugging.

20. How do you ensure zero-downtime deployments in Kubernetes?

Rolling Updates: Kubernetes supports rolling updates, which allow for zero-downtime deployments. When a Deployment is updated, Kubernetes updates the Pods one at a time, ensuring that some Pods are always available to serve traffic.

To ensure zero downtime:

Set sufficient replicas: Make sure you have multiple replicas of your application running. This will ensure that there is always at least one Pod serving traffic.

Example of ensuring enough replicas during updates:

spec:
  replicas: 3
Configure maxUnavailable and maxSurge: The Deployment strategy has two key parameters to control the behavior of rolling updates:
maxUnavailable: Specifies how many Pods can be unavailable during the update.
maxSurge: Specifies how many extra Pods can be created during the update.

Example configuration for zero-downtime updates:

spec:
  strategy:
    rollingUpdate:
      maxUnavailable: 1
      maxSurge: 1

This ensures that at least one Pod is always running and the update process will create additional Pods if needed before deleting the old ones.

Health Checks (Probes): Set up proper liveness and readiness probes to ensure that only healthy Pods are part of the load balancer pool and serve traffic. If a new Pod fails its readiness probe, Kubernetes won’t route traffic to it.
Blue-Green or Canary Deployments: For more advanced zero-downtime deployments, consider using Blue-Green or Canary deployment strategies. With Blue-Green, you have two environments (Blue and Green), and you switch traffic to the new version once it is ready. Canary deployments roll out the update to a small portion of users first, before gradually expanding it to the rest.

Kubernetes doesn’t natively support Blue-Green, but tools like Helm or ArgoCD can be used to manage these kinds of deployments.

Monitoring during Deployment: During updates, use monitoring tools like Prometheus and Grafana to track the health of the system and identify any issues immediately if the new Pods aren’t healthy.
